#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bessel_i

#% text
# `bessel_i(nu, x)` gives the modified Bessel function of the first kind `I_nu(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `I_nu(x)` solves the modified Bessel equation `x^2 y'' + x y' - (x^2 + nu^2) y = 0` and is regular at 0 (DLMF 10.25.2); it grows like `exp(x)/sqrt(2*pi*x)`.

#% item
# Exact values at 0 and for half-integer orders; machine numbers and balls give numerical values.

#% item
# `bessel_i` is listable in both arguments.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
bessel_i(0, 1.0)
#% output
#= 1.2660658777520084

#% cell
bessel_i(1, 2.5)
#% output
#= 2.5167162452886984

#% cell
bessel_i(0, 0)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
bessel_i(1/2, x)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)*sinh(x)/(pi*x)^(1/2)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# 30 digits:

#% cell
n(bessel_i(0, 1), 30)
#% output
#= 1.2660658777520083355982446252147175

#% text
# A fractional order and a large argument:

#% cell
bessel_i(2.5, 3.0)
#% output
#= 1.5153394466819652

#% cell
bessel_i(0, 100.0)
#% output
#= 1.0737517071310738e42

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `I_0(x)` is `J_0(i x)`, the series with all signs positive:

#% cell
bessel_i(0, 1.0) - sum(0.25^k/factorial(k)^2, [k, 0, 30])
#% output
#= 2.220446049250313e-16

#% text
# The derivative of `I_0`, the mean of the neighbouring orders (which are equal, `I_(-1) = I_1`):

#% cell
d(bessel_i(0, x), x)
#% output
#= (1/2)*(bessel_i(-1, x) + bessel_i(1, x))

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `bessel_k`, `bessel_j`, `hypergeometric_0f1`, `kelvin_ber`
